  if not exists (select 1 from public.blog_posts where id = '4a0f69a6-6b8b-4c48-935b-df594d74d395'::uuid and slug = 'website-for-architects-mumbai-2026' and md5(content) = 'ede1e5e7251621772f44ff2ec0414038' and title = 'Do Architects in Mumbai Need a Website in 2026?' and status = 'published' and updated_at is not distinct from '2026-09-25 07:19:40.609+00'::timestamptz for update) then
    raise exception 'Editorial source changed: website-for-architects-mumbai-2026';
  end if;
  update public.blog_posts set title = 'Architecture Portfolio Engineering: Projects, Galleries and Commission Enquiries', excerpt = 'Plan project narratives, responsive images, stable galleries and private drawing delivery for an architecture practice.', content = '<p>A prospective client opens an architect''s project gallery on a phone while travelling between meetings. The photographs are impressive, but the page jumps as each image arrives. There is no explanation of whether the practice designed the building, its interiors or only the façade. The visitor leaves with an aesthetic impression and no reliable basis for commissioning work. That is an information-design problem before it is a marketing problem.</p>
<p>For a Mumbai architecture practice, the website should make the work legible: what the brief was, what constraints shaped it, what the practice delivered and how an appropriate enquiry begins. This guide describes a proposed implementation approach, not a claim about measured results for an architecture client.</p>
<h2>Make each project a readable design argument</h2>
<p>Start with a small number of documented projects rather than an enormous image wall. Give each project a stable URL, a descriptive title and a concise account of the commission. Building type, broad location, stage and the practice''s actual role help a reader interpret the images. Distinguish a completed building from an unbuilt proposal and a competition entry. A rendering should be labelled as a rendering, especially when it sits beside construction photographs.</p>
<p>Write about constraints the team can substantiate. A compact site, an occupied renovation, a phased construction schedule or a particular daylight requirement gives the reader something to evaluate. Describe the design response in ordinary language. Avoid inventing energy savings, budget figures or client approval quotations merely to make the case study look complete. If an outcome was not measured, show the design decision and its limitation instead.</p>
<p>Project credits matter too. Identify collaborators and photography permissions, and agree what may be published before preparing the gallery. Residential clients may not want an exact address, detailed floor plan or recognisable personal possessions online. A broad neighbourhood and selected drawings can communicate architectural thinking without exposing the household. Public portfolio access and confidential project delivery should use separate systems.</p>
<h2>Build a gallery that stays still while images arrive</h2>
<p>Architecture imagery often has mixed aspect ratios. Generate several image widths and use responsive image markup so a narrow phone does not receive the same file as a large desktop. Offer AVIF or WebP where your image pipeline supports them, retain a compatible fallback, and inspect fine drawings for compression damage. A smaller file is useful only if captions and important design details remain readable.</p>
<p>Set width and height attributes or a deliberate aspect ratio for every gallery tile. The browser can then reserve space before the image downloads, reducing layout movement. A masonry effect should not continually rearrange already visible work as dimensions become known. A simple grid with predictable rows is often easier to browse, navigate with a keyboard and maintain than an elaborate layout whose visual order differs from its reading order.</p>
<p>Lazy-load images below the opening viewport. The main project image, if it is the Largest Contentful Paint candidate, should be discoverable in the initial HTML and should not be lazily loaded. Measure the actual candidate before assigning high fetch priority. Giving every image high priority merely makes them compete. Google''s <a href="https://web.dev/articles/optimize-lcp">LCP guidance</a> explains why discovery time and resource priority need to be considered together.</p>
<h2>Separate the public portfolio from drawing delivery</h2>
<p>A Cloudflare CDN can cache public, versioned images close to visitors, but it does not make a confidential drawing safe to publish. Give public image files content-based or versioned names so a revised image can receive a new URL. Avoid putting client documents behind a guessable path and treating the absence of navigation as access control. A public bucket remains public even if no menu links to it.</p>
<p>For private files, use authenticated access or short-lived authorised links and appropriate cache headers. Check the actual response through the delivery path rather than assuming that an application setting controls every intermediary. Cloudflare documents how <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/concepts/cache-control/">origin cache directives</a> affect shared caching. A practice should decide who can access drawings, how long access lasts and what happens when a project ends.</p>
<h2>Ask questions that establish whether the commission fits</h2>
<p>An architecture enquiry should establish project type, broad site location, approximate scale, stage and the service being requested. A person seeking an interiors refresh should not accidentally submit a new-build brief. Keep approximate budget and schedule questions clear about their purpose, and permit uncertainty where the visitor is still investigating feasibility. Do not request title documents or identity records through the first marketing form.</p>
<p>Send an explicit acknowledgement after the server has accepted the request. Explain that the team will assess fit and availability; submission is not acceptance of the commission. Record a minimal enquiry reference so a visitor can discuss the request without resending the entire brief. Notify the practice through a reliable channel, and test what happens if notification delivery is delayed after the enquiry itself has been stored.</p>
<h2>Commission the content system, not just its opening screen</h2>
<p>Before approving the build, ask a staff member to add a project using real photographs, a long title and incomplete metadata. Check that they can reorder images, add credits, remove a withdrawn photograph and change the project stage without developer intervention. Confirm how redirects are maintained when titles or slugs change. A portfolio becomes stale quickly if updating it requires editing source code that the practice does not own or understand.</p>
<p>Review the final site on a narrow phone with a cold cache and a slower connection. Navigate project pages without relying on hover, open the enquiry form using a keyboard, and confirm that image alternatives describe meaningful content rather than repeat search phrases. The result should let a potential client understand the practice''s decisions and make a well-informed approach. That is a stronger brief than simply asking whether architects need another online presence.</p>
', tags = ARRAY['Architecture', 'Web Design', 'Performance']::text[], blanks_metadata = '[]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where id = '4a0f69a6-6b8b-4c48-935b-df594d74d395'::uuid;
end $repair$;

do $repair$ begin
  if not exists (select 1 from public.blog_posts where id = '2debabd8-7a95-466c-97e0-b03d90407b9b'::uuid and slug = '5-things-every-ca-firm-website-must-have' and md5(content) = 'fd884ce3660ecd4b8cb7f6af15562c51' and title = '5 Things Every CA Firm Website Must Have' and status = 'published' and updated_at is not distinct from '2026-09-16 09:50:57.335868+00'::timestamptz for update) then
    raise exception 'Editorial source changed: 5-things-every-ca-firm-website-must-have';
  end if;
  update public.blog_posts set title = 'A CA Firm Website: Professional Review, Enquiry Routing and Document Boundaries', excerpt = 'A practical architecture for factual firm information, reviewed preparation checklists and controlled client-document workflows.', content = '<p>A client asks a CA firm which documents are needed for an engagement. One partner replies by email, another sends an old checklist and the office receives sensitive attachments on a general enquiry address. The website can help organise this workflow, but only if its public information and its confidential client process are designed separately.</p>
<p>For a Mumbai chartered accountancy practice, the useful brief is a factual professional website with controlled enquiry routing. It should explain scope without unsupported promotional claims, help people prepare for the next conversation and avoid turning a brochure form into an improvised document vault. The examples below are proposed workflows, not legal advice or a statement that a particular firm complies with professional rules.</p>
<h2>Put professional review ahead of the visual concept</h2>
<p>ICAI regulates how members present professional information. Before writing promotional copy or installing a third-party widget, have the responsible member review the current Code of Ethics, applicable website guidance and any relevant announcements. Start from <a href="https://www.icai.org/">ICAI''s official website</a>, rather than relying on an agency''s summary of an old handbook. Record which version was checked and who approved the final content. This guide does not reproduce a supposedly exhaustive list of permitted claims.</p>
<p>Make that review concrete. Create an inventory containing partner descriptions, qualifications, service wording, logos, testimonials, rankings, advertisements, referral arrangements and outbound links. Mark each item as approved, needing changes or excluded. A developer should not infer permission from the fact that another firm''s website uses the same pattern. Design choices can also create claims: a badge saying best tax expert is still a statement even if it appears inside an image.</p>
<p>Keep the published identity consistent. The firm name, actual offices, partner details and contact instructions should be verified by the practice. Do not imply that a correspondence address is a staffed branch. If services depend on engagement acceptance or another professional''s involvement, explain that boundary factually. Content review is a maintained operating responsibility, not a checkbox completed once at launch.</p>
<h2>Route an enquiry without soliciting a file cabinet</h2>
<p>A first enquiry can ask for the broad matter, business type, preferred contact method and deadline. It usually does not need PAN scans, bank statements, passwords or complete tax returns. Put an explicit instruction near the form asking visitors not to send confidential records at this stage. The team can then direct an accepted client to an appropriate document process after establishing scope and authority.</p>
<p>Use server-side validation and a finite set of routing categories, with an option for an uncertain enquiry. Do not let a browser select an arbitrary recipient address or internal workflow. Store the accepted request before showing confirmation, and avoid including the visitor''s narrative in analytics events. If notification fails, retain the enquiry and surface the delivery problem to the office rather than silently losing the request.</p>
<h2>Make preparation checklists explicit about assumptions</h2>
<p>An automated checklist is useful when it explains its limits. A visitor can select the service they want to discuss and receive a preparation list based on a versioned template approved by the firm. The page should state that the final document request depends on the engagement and professional review. It should not suggest that a short questionnaire establishes tax treatment, eligibility or the completeness of a filing.</p>
<p>Maintain checklist content centrally. Save an internal template version and review date so staff can recognise an outdated download. If a rule or process changes, revise the source and review affected pages instead of patching several unrelated PDFs. A public checklist can describe categories of information to have ready; the confidential documents themselves belong in a separate controlled exchange.</p>
<p>Consider a simple example: someone asks about bookkeeping support for a small trading company. The public page can explain the information needed to understand transaction volume and existing systems. It should not request accounting-platform passwords. A staff member reviews the enquiry, agrees the next step and provides a suitable invitation to the selected system. This preserves a useful public journey while avoiding an unsafe shortcut.</p>
<h2>Protect the boundary between marketing and client work</h2>
<p>A client portal needs more than a login screen. Decide which staff can read each engagement, how users are removed, how access is logged and how records are retained. Test authorisation using a second client account: changing an identifier must not reveal another client''s files. An authenticated user is not automatically authorised to access every record. Keep public marketing content and client records in clearly separated access paths.</p>
<p>Private pages and downloads should not be stored by shared caches. Configure responses deliberately and inspect them through the real hosting path. Never put confidential record identifiers, document contents or signed file links into analytics parameters. Error reports also need review because an otherwise helpful diagnostic can accidentally capture form input or an attachment URL. The principle is to collect enough evidence to diagnose failure without copying the client''s case into unrelated systems.</p>
<h2>Agree on ownership for the next compliance update</h2>
<p>Assign a firm reviewer to service descriptions, deadlines, professional identity and checklist templates. Let the developer own implementation checks such as link behaviour, access controls and form recovery. Both roles need an escalation path: a technically functioning page can contain outdated professional information, while approved prose can still sit above an insecure upload control.</p>
<p>At handover, test a normal enquiry, an invalid submission, a duplicate click and a temporary notification outage. Check keyboard navigation and readable form errors on a phone. Ask the practice to update a partner description and withdraw an obsolete checklist using the actual editor. These are practical acceptance tasks that reveal whether the website will remain accurate after launch.</p>
<p>The best starting point may be a small factual site with a carefully routed enquiry form. Add document automation only when the firm has agreed the security, professional-review and maintenance requirements. A restrained system that staff can explain and operate is more useful than a long feature list that transfers sensitive work into an unreviewed marketing tool.</p>
', tags = ARRAY['Professional Services', 'Web Design', 'Security']::text[], blanks_metadata = '[]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where id = '2debabd8-7a95-466c-97e0-b03d90407b9b'::uuid;
end $repair$;

do $repair$ begin
  if not exists (select 1 from public.blog_posts where id = 'acc27b79-56a7-473a-bbc8-74f9e4d40336'::uuid and slug = '5-things-every-salon-spa-website-needs' and md5(content) = '0c79b9b5d889e4c7620a6e8377d2b0ac' and title = '5 Things Every Salon & Spa Website in Mumbai Needs' and status = 'published' and updated_at is not distinct from '2026-09-16 09:50:57.335868+00'::timestamptz for update) then
    raise exception 'Editorial source changed: 5-things-every-salon-spa-website-needs';
  end if;
  update public.blog_posts set title = 'Salon Website Booking Design: Services, Staff and Honest Availability', excerpt = 'Design a salon service menu and appointment journey that distinguishes requests from reservations and handles scheduling conflicts.', content = '<p>A customer selects a haircut, chooses a stylist and asks for Saturday afternoon. The website says booking complete, but the salon has only received an email. The stylist is already occupied and the customer arrives expecting a confirmed slot. This mismatch is a more serious website problem than an unremarkable colour palette.</p>
<p>A salon or spa website should explain services and represent availability honestly. The right implementation depends on how the team schedules work, whether different staff perform different treatments and how consultation, preparation and cleanup affect the calendar. Start with those operational facts before choosing a booking plugin.</p>
<h2>A service is more than its name and starting price</h2>
<p>A usable service menu distinguishes duration, staff eligibility and the conditions affecting price. Hair colour may depend on length, product and consultation; a spa treatment may need preparation time and a room as well as a therapist. Present the information the business can maintain, and state when a consultation is required before the final service is agreed. Avoid showing a misleading single duration for work that cannot reliably fit that slot.</p>
<p>Organise the menu into meaningful categories with stable links. A customer should be able to share one service without sending a screenshot of an enormous poster. Text prices and descriptions work better with mobile zoom, assistive technology and search than a menu embedded only as an image. An optional visual price sheet can supplement the page, but it should not be the only way to understand a service.</p>
<h2>Choose between a request and a real reservation</h2>
<p>A request form is appropriate when staff need to check suitability or arrange availability manually. Its confirmation should say request received and explain how the salon will respond. A calendar that genuinely reserves slots needs a server-side source of availability, not just a browser timetable. If a staff member books the same time by phone, the website must see that change before confirming another customer.</p>
<p>Model resources explicitly. A treatment may require a particular therapist and a room; another may require a colour station followed by a wash station. Include buffers where the business needs preparation or cleanup. The calendar should calculate service end times using those rules and the salon''s local timezone. Opening hours alone do not prove that the required staff and equipment are free.</p>
<p>When two customers select the last slot, only one should receive a confirmed reservation. The booking backend needs an atomic allocation or an equivalent conflict check at commit time. Reusing the same submission identifier should return the original result rather than create another appointment. If a connection drops after confirmation, refreshing the page should recover the existing booking rather than encourage an accidental duplicate.</p>
<h2>Keep messaging useful without pretending it is scheduling</h2>
<p>A WhatsApp click-to-chat link can prefill a service name and preferred time. It should not put sensitive treatment details or private photographs into a public URL. Make it clear that opening WhatsApp does not reserve a slot. Some visitors will not send the message after the app opens, so the salon should not count every click as an appointment.</p>
<p>If deposits are collected, separate the appointment state from the payment state. A browser success screen is not evidence that money was captured, and a captured payment does not prove the correct stylist remains available. Define how a slot is held while checkout is in progress and what happens when payment is delayed. The cancellation and refund process must match the salon''s published terms and actual booking system.</p>
<h2>Show work without slowing the service menu</h2>
<p>Before-and-after galleries can help someone understand a stylist''s work when the images are accurate and permission to publish is recorded. Avoid editing that misrepresents a result. Label the service or technique in ordinary language and avoid treating one photograph as a guarantee of the same outcome for everyone. Remove identifying information where the customer has not agreed to publication.</p>
<p>Create responsive image sizes and compressed WebP or AVIF variants where suitable. Reserve each image''s layout space with dimensions or an aspect ratio. Load the visible lead image promptly, while images further down the gallery can be lazy-loaded. A social-feed widget should not block the service menu or the booking control. See Google''s <a href="https://web.dev/articles/optimize-cls">layout-shift guidance</a> for why reserved image space matters.</p>
<h2>Make changes and cancellations findable</h2>
<p>A confirmation should identify the service, salon location, requested or confirmed time and the next action. If the booking platform provides a private change link, keep it out of public analytics and do not expose other customers'' appointments through sequential identifiers. Customers need to understand whether they can reschedule directly or must contact staff. A vague contact us footer is poor recovery when someone needs to change tomorrow''s appointment.</p>
<p>Staff also need a clear view of requests awaiting action. Define who checks the queue, what happens outside operating hours and how the website communicates delayed replies. Avoid an automatic promise of a response within minutes unless the salon has a process that consistently supports it. Honest expectations reduce confusion more effectively than another decorative urgency message.</p>
<h2>Rehearse a busy Saturday before launch</h2>
<p>Use test bookings to simulate two customers choosing the same time, a stylist becoming unavailable, a multi-service visit and a customer returning after closing the tab. Check that no duplicate appointment appears and that the wording remains accurate through each state. Test a mobile keyboard, long customer names and someone using a screen reader to navigate the date picker.</p>
<p>Then ask the receptionist to change a service duration and publish an unexpected closure. If the site and booking system disagree afterward, the integration needs more work. A useful salon website makes the team easier to work with while preserving an honest account of availability. That operational consistency is the foundation; galleries and promotions can support it once the scheduling journey works.</p>
', tags = ARRAY['Booking', 'Web Design', 'User Experience']::text[], blanks_metadata = '[]'::jsonb, updated_at = now() where id = 'acc27b79-56a7-473a-bbc8-74f9e4d40336'::uuid;
end $repair$;

